# Exercise: Ping-Pong
#
# Two strands exchanging messages over two channels.
#
# Strand A (main) sends "ping" on the ping channel.
# Strand B (worker) receives ping, sends "pong" on the pong
# channel. Strand A receives pong.
#
# Same closure-by-copy capture as before, but now the worker
# quotation needs *two* channels. The typechecker counts how
# many inputs the body needs and captures that many from the
# top of the stack at literal time — so with `( ping pong )` on
# the stack, `[ body ] strand.spawn` captures both and leaves
# both untouched on main's stack.
#
# Your task: send 0 ("ping") to the ping channel, receive 1
# ("pong") from the pong channel.
#
# When done, delete the marker line below.

# I AM NOT DONE

: test-ping-pong ( -- )
    chan.make           # ( ping )
    chan.make           # ( ping pong )
    [
        # closure stack: ( ping pong )
        swap chan.receive drop drop   # receive ping, drop value+flag
        1 swap chan.send drop         # send pong (1)
    ] strand.spawn drop                # ( ping pong )

    over 0 swap chan.send drop         # send 0 to ping: ( ping pong )

    # Your code here — receive from pong (top of stack), drop the
    # flag, and drop the leftover ping reference so only the value
    # remains.
    drop drop
    0
    # Do not edit below this line
    1 test.assert-eq
;
